{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וכי תזבחו", :translit "v'chi tizbachu", :gloss "and when you slaughter"} {:he "לאותו ואת בנו", :translit "l'oto v'et b'no", :gloss "to 'it and its son'"} {:he "בד' פרקים בשנה", :translit "b'arba p'rakim ba-shanah", :gloss "in the four periods of the year"} {:he "אמו מכרתי היום לשחוט", :translit "imo micharti ha-yom lishchot", :gloss "its mother [I have] sold it today to slaughter"} {:he "לרגלים", :translit "l'regalim", :gloss "to the festivals"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of our verse to the preceding verse (Lev 22:28, 'it and its son') to the subsequent verse (Lev 23, festivals)"}], :frame {:text "The preceding verse Leviticus 22:28 prohibits slaughtering an animal 'it and its son' (אותו ואת בנו) on the same day. The Mishnah applies this specifically to a kosher slaughter, and the Gemara at Chullin 82b states: 'In four periods of the year, one who sells an animal must notify the buyer: I sold it today to slaughter,' these being the eve of the last day of each festival when people need meat. Because Leviticus 22:29 directly follows the prohibition of 'it and its son' and immediately precedes Leviticus 23's festival calendar, the Ba'al HaTurim reads the juxtaposition as alluding to this halakhah that links the prohibition's practical urgency to the festival seasons.", :sources ["Leviticus 22:28" "Leviticus 22:29" "Leviticus 23" "Chullin 82b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of our verse to the preceding verse (Lev 22:28, 'it and its son') to the subsequent verse (Lev 23, festivals)", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:25:02.726517711Z", :model "glm-5.2"}